# Accepted dependency vulnerabilities — SEC-1.
#
# pip-audit has no native ignore-file format, so this file IS the format.
# .github/workflows/security.yml reads it in both audit steps: comments and
# blank lines are stripped, and the first token of each remaining line becomes
# one `--ignore-vuln <ID>` argument.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An entry here silences a real, published vulnerability. It is not a to-do
# list and it is not a place to park a red build. Every entry MUST carry all
# five fields below, on the comment lines directly above it:
#
#   Package     name==version, exactly as pip-audit printed it
#   Why         why this vulnerability is not reachable in THIS product —
#               a named import path or the absence of one, not "low severity"
#   Fix         the version that fixes it, and why we are not simply taking it
#   Accepted    who accepted it, and on what date
#   Review by   a date. An exception with no expiry is a permanent hole.
#
# Format (the ID must be the first token on the line):
#
#   # Package:   somepkg==1.2.3
#   # Why:       the vulnerable code path is <module>.<func>; nothing under
#   #            src/vib_agent/ imports it — grep confirms test-only use.
#   # Fix:       1.2.4, blocked on <reason>.
#   # Accepted:  <name>, 2026-09-05
#   # Review by: 2026-12-05
#   GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx
#
# Prefer, in order: upgrade the package; drop the dependency; then, only if
# neither is possible, add it here. An accepted exception that outlives its
# review date should be re-argued, not renewed by inertia.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# NO ENTRIES. The file ships empty of exceptions deliberately — a pre-populated
# ignore list is a set of holes nobody argued for. See outputs/SESSION_SEC1.md
# for the one finding this session surfaced (pypdf in constraints.txt) and the
# reason it was fixed at the pin rather than silenced here.
